Long ago, when the sky was an endless void of darkness, there lived a lonely weaver named Celesta. She dwelled at the edge of the world, where earth met the empty heavens, and spent her days spinning thread from moonlight and shadow.

One night, Celesta gazed into the black expanse and wept, for she had no one to share her creations with. Her tears fell upward into the void, and where each droplet landed, it froze into a tiny point of light.

Amazed, Celesta began to work. She took her finest silver thread and cast it into the darkness, weaving patterns and pictures across the sky. She stitched hunters and bears, swans and dragons, crowns and harps. Each knot she tied became a brilliant star, each constellation a story.

She worked for a thousand nights, filling the emptiness with her celestial tapestry. When she finally stepped back to admire her work, she smiled, for she was no longer alone. Every creature on earth could now look up and see her stories shimmering above them.

